From vManager, go into vShell:

On the vManager console, after initial configuration, go into shell mode. 
# vshell

The following creates a root CA from the vManager and is the issuer.
% openssl genrsa -out SDWAN.key 2048

openssl req -x509 -new -nodes -key SDWAN.key -sha256 -days 2000 -subj "/C=UK/ST=London/L=NorthWest/O=thebiglab/CN=thebiglab" -out SDWAN.pem

% cat SDWAN.key <-- this will show the base64 certificate
% cat SDWAN.pem <-- this will show the csr certificate

% cat sdwan.pem 
% copy the start and end of that file


Note:----
 If you want to stop vManage from contacting Cisco’s cloud CA (Cisco SUDI / Cisco PKI)
CML, lab builds, and non‑production SD‑WAN deployments often do not use Cisco’s cloud CA.
To disable Cisco CA integration:
✔️ Disable Cisco PKI / Cisco Cloud CA in vManage
In vManage → Administration → Settings → Controller Certificate Authorization:
• Set Controller Certificate Authorization to Manual
• Disable Cisco Automated Certificate Signing
• Remove any Cisco Smart Account / Virtual Account entries
• Remove Cisco CA URL if present
----


 % openssl x509 -req -in vbond.csr -CA SDWAN.pem -CAkey SDWAN.key -CAcreateserial -out NewCertificate.crt -days 2000 -sha256

Then go onto vmanager web GUI and install the certificate.


On each of the devices once the root-ca is applied (SDWAN.pem), create a csr for each as below via vManger GUI.  
This can be done on the cli of each device by:

# vshell

% openssl x509 -req -in  vmanage_csr -CA SDWAN.pem -CAkey SDWAN.key -CAcreateserial -out NewCertificate.crt -days 2000 -sha256

% openssl x509 -req -in  vbond_csr -CA SDWAN.pem -CAkey SDWAN.key -CAcreateserial -out NewCertificate.crt -days 2000 -sha256

% openssl x509 -req -in  vsmart_csr -CA SDWAN.pem -CAkey SDWAN.key -CAcreateserial -out NewCertificate.crt -days 2000 -sha256


